  ${
    ist1-5lem.1 $e |- ( J e. A -> J e. Kol2 ) $.
    ist1-5lem.2 $e |- ( J ~= ( KQ ` J ) ->
      ( J e. A -> ( KQ ` J ) e. A ) ) $.
    ist1-5lem.3 $e |- ( ( KQ ` J ) ~= J ->
      ( ( KQ ` J ) e. A -> J e. A ) ) $.
    $( Lemma for ~ ist1-5 and similar theorems.  If ` A ` is a topological
       property which implies T_0, such as T_1 or T_2, the property can be
       "decomposed" into T_0 and a non-T_0 version of property ` A ` (which is
       defined as stating that the Kolmogorov quotient of the space has
       property ` A ` ).  For example, if ` A ` is T_1, then the theorem states
       that a space is T_1 iff it is T_0 and its Kolmogorov quotient is T_1 (we
       call this property R_0).  (Contributed by Mario Carneiro,
       25-Aug-2015.) $)
    ist1-5lem $p |- ( J e. A <-> ( J e. Kol2 /\ ( KQ ` J ) e. A ) ) $=
      ( wcel ct0 ckq cfv wa chmph wbr kqhmph sylib mpcom jca hmphsym sylbi syl
      wi imp impbii ) BAFZBGFZBHIZAFZJUCUDUFCBUEKLZUCUFUCUDUGCBMZNDOPUDUFUCUDUE
      BKLZUFUCTUDUGUIUHBUEQRESUAUB $.
  $}

  $( A T_1 space is R_0.  That is, the Kolmogorov quotient of a T_1 space is
     also T_1 (because they are homeomorphic).  (Contributed by Mario Carneiro,
     25-Aug-2015.) $)
  t1r0 $p |- ( J e. Fre -> ( KQ ` J ) e. Fre ) $=
    ( ckq cfv chmph wbr ct1 wcel ct0 t1t0 kqhmph sylib t1hmph mpcom ) AABCZDEZA
    FGZNFGPAHGOAIAJKANLM $.

  $( A topological space is T_1 iff it is both T_0 and R_0.  (Contributed by
     Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.) $)
  ist1-5 $p |- ( J e. Fre <-> ( J e. Kol2 /\ ( KQ ` J ) e. Fre ) ) $=
    ( ct1 t1t0 ckq cfv t1hmph ist1-5lem ) BAACAADEZFHAFG $.

  $( A topological space is Hausdorff iff it is both T_0 and R_1 (where R_1
     means that any two topologically distinct points are separated by
     neighborhoods).  (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.) $)
  ishaus3 $p |- ( J e. Haus <-> ( J e. Kol2 /\ ( KQ ` J ) e. Haus ) ) $=
    ( cha wcel ct1 ct0 haust1 t1t0 syl ckq cfv haushmph ist1-5lem ) BAABCADCAEC
    AFAGHAAIJZKMAKL $.

  $( A normal T_1 space is regular Hausdorff.  In other words, a T_4 space is
     T_3 .  One can get away with slightly weaker assumptions; see ~ nrmr0reg .
     (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.) $)
